Links — the relationships.

If hubs are the entities, links are the relationships and transactions between them. A link records that two or more hubs are connected — and, like a hub, it holds keys only, with no descriptive data.

So what's in a link? Take link order-line, which connects an order to a product — which is exactly the sale-line grain. It has the order-line hash key — the primary key, computed as a hash of the order hash key plus the product hash key. It has the order hash key, a foreign key to hub order. It has the product hash key, a foreign key to hub product. And it has the standard load date and record source. Similarly, link order-customer connects an order to the customer who placed it — order hash key plus customer hash key. A link is just the hash keys of the hubs it ties together, plus the standard metadata. No attributes, no measures.

Now here's the golden rule, the design decision that gives Data Vault its agility: model every relationship as many-to-many, even if today it looks one-to-many. Because a link imposes no cardinality, the relationship can change — one order gaining many shipments, a product appearing on many orders — without ever restructuring the model. In a star, a cardinality change can mean rebuilding a fact. In a vault, the link already allows it.

So where does the relationship's own data go? A link carries no descriptive columns — so the measures and attributes of the relationship live in a satellite on the link. Link order-line has no numbers itself; its sat sales-measures satellite holds the quantity, the unit price, the line total. And satellites are the very next section. So think of a link as the vault's analogue of a fact's key-set, or of a bridge table: it captures that things relate; the satellite captures the details. In short: a link records a relationship between hubs, using their hash keys only, and it's always modelled many-to-many — so relationships can evolve without redesign. Its measures ride in a satellite hung off it.
